.\" ============================ ABSTRACT ============================
.in +0.5i
.ll -0.5i
.ps 9
.vs 11.5p
.ft B
Abstract
.ft R
.sp 0.3v
Deriving a program\(cqs source, documentation and tests from one authoritative
specification is an old idea with at least five largely independent lineages:
formal refinement, model-driven engineering, interface definition languages,
in-project code generation, and the agent-oriented \(lqspec-driven development\(rq
that emerged in 2025. This paper surveys the five, reports what is known about
how widely each is practised and in which languages, and argues that they share
a failure mode that none of their literatures names directly. Derivation
guarantees fidelity
.I downstream
of the specification and guarantees nothing about fidelity
.I into
it. Because every derived artefact agrees with the specification by
construction, a defect at the root produces no disagreement anywhere in the
tree \(em it is instead multiplied across every generated artefact that asserts
it. This is structurally the correlated-failure result that Knight and Leveson
established for N-version programming in 1986, arrived at from the opposite
direction: N-version programming lost independence it assumed it had, whereas a
derived tree discards independence deliberately, in exchange for consistency.
We illustrate with a defect from the authors\(cq own codebase in which six
generated artefacts agreed on a wrong rule for four months, and the only
dissenting voices were two hand-written documents that nothing checked.
.ps \n(PS
.vs 12.5p
.in -0.5i
.ll +0.5i
.sp 1.2v
.
.\" ============================ 1 ============================
.S1 1.  Introduction
.LP
A project that generates its command-line parser, its manual page, its error
constants and its test matrix from a single declarative file is doing something
that has a name \(em several names, in fact, belonging to communities that rarely
cite one another. The practical question this paper began from was narrow: is
this a recognised engineering paradigm, how common is it, and who else does it?
.PP
The short answer is that it is a recognised
.I mechanism
with no single recognised
.I name .
Five traditions have converged on generating artefacts from an authoritative
description, for reasons ranging from mathematical proof to reducing boilerplate
in a compiler backend. They differ in what the specification is written in, what
is generated from it, which direction the derivation runs, and what guarantee is
claimed. What unites them is not a lineage but a structure, and it is the
structure that carries the failure mode described in Section 8.
.PP
Section 2 states the method and its limits. Section 3 examines what the term
denotes today, which is narrower and much more recent than the practice.
Sections 4 through 7 survey the traditions, their prevalence, their languages,
and a cross-cutting axis \(em the direction of derivation \(em that complicates
the \(lqsingle source of truth\(rq slogan. Section 8 develops the shared failure
mode and connects it to an established result. Section 9 gives a worked example.
Sections 10 and 11 draw practical consequences and identify what the literature
does not cover.
.
.\" ============================ 2 ============================
.S1 2.  Method and limitations
.LP
This is a survey assembled from public sources over a single working session,
not a systematic literature review. It was not conducted with a registered
protocol, a defined search string set, or inter-rater agreement, and it makes no
claim to exhaustiveness. Where adoption figures are quoted they come from
industry surveys with self-selected respondents, whose absolute values should be
treated as indicative rather than measured.
.PP
One methodological note is worth recording because it affected the survey
itself. Search-engine summarisation of this topic proved unreliable in a
specific way: a summary offered a confident provenance for the term \(em tracing
it to \(lq1960s NASA workflows\(rq and an \(lqacademic formalisation in
2004\(rq \(em of which only the 2004 citation survived contact with the source
document. The cited article contains no NASA claim and no discussion of the
modern wave at all. Popular writing on specification-driven development is
unusually prone to inherited, unsourced history. Every claim of provenance below
was checked against a primary document, and where a claim could not be so
checked it has been omitted rather than hedged.
.PP
The case study in Section 9 is the authors\(cq own project. It is offered as an
existence proof and an illustration of a mechanism, not as evidence of
frequency. A single incident in a single small codebase cannot establish how
often the failure mode occurs; it can only show concretely what it looks like
and why the surrounding machinery did not catch it.
.
.\" ============================ 3 ============================
.S1 3.  What the name denotes
.S2 3.1  The academic thread
.LP
As a term of art, \(lqspecification-driven development\(rq has a thin academic
record. The encyclopaedic entry for it is a stub which classifies it as a form
of documentation-driven development alongside model-driven development, model
transformation and round-trip engineering, and which rests on a single
substantive citation: Ostroff, Makalsky and Paige\(cqs
.I "Agile Specification-Driven Development"
(XP 2004) [1]. That paper is narrower than the name suggests. It argues that
tests and contracts are two kinds of specification, useful and complementary,
and it makes the argument in Eiffel, where Design by Contract is a language
feature. It is a synthesis of test-driven development and contract-based
design \(em not a theory of generating a system from a description.
.PP
This matters for anyone searching the literature. The phrase does not retrieve
the tradition most practitioners of the mechanism actually belong to. Someone
generating a parser from a schema will find almost nothing under this name and a
great deal under \(lqmodel-driven engineering,\(rq \(lqcode generation,\(rq
\(lqinterface definition language\(rq or \(lqlanguage workbench.\(rq
.
.S2 3.2  The 2025 wave
.LP
Since mid-2025 the term has been claimed, largely successfully, by a different
practice: using a written specification as the durable artefact that a large
language model generates code from. Amazon\(cqs Kiro (July 2025) structures work
into requirements, design and task stages. GitHub\(cqs Spec Kit, open-sourced in
September 2025, adds orchestration and a \(lqconstitution\(rq of immutable
principles. Tessl goes furthest, proposing that the specification rather than the
code be the maintained artefact \(em \(lqspec-as-source.\(rq
.PP
The honest current status of this wave is cautious. Thoughtworks placed
spec-driven development in the
.B Assess
ring of its Technology Radar in November 2025 [2] \(em \(lqworth exploring with
the goal of understanding how it will affect your enterprise\(rq \(em with
material reservations:
.QS
\(lqthe workflows remain elaborate and opinionated. These tools behave very
differently depending on task size and type; some generate lengthy spec files
that are hard to review \&... when they produce PRDs or user stories, it\(cqs
sometimes unclear who their intended user is.\(rq
.QE
.LP
It would be an error \(em the easiest error this subject invites \(em to read
that assessment as applying to the other four traditions, or to read their
maturity as applying to it. A tool ecosystem eight months old and a code
generator with thirty years of production service in a compiler are not the same
proposition merely because both begin from a file.
.
.\" ============================ 4 ============================
.S1 4.  Five traditions
.LP
The following table summarises the five. The rows are ordered by age, not by
importance or prevalence.
.sp 0.5v
.TS
allbox tab(|);
lb  lb    lb        lb
l   l     l         l.
Tradition|Specification is|Generated|Guarantee claimed
_
T{
Formal
refinement
T}|T{
A logic or ISA
description language
T}|T{
Provers, simulators,
tests
T}|T{
Machine-checked
correctness
T}
T{
Model-driven
engineering
T}|T{
A model or a
bespoke DSL
T}|T{
Application code,
schemas
T}|T{
Productivity,
consistency
T}
T{
Interface
definition
T}|T{
A wire or API
schema
T}|T{
Clients, servers,
documentation
T}|Interoperability
T{
In-project
generation
T}|T{
A build-time DSL
T}|T{
Source, documentation,
tables
T}|Non-duplication
T{
