(under assessment)
T}
.TE
.sp 0.5v
.
.S2 4.1  Formal specification and refinement
.LP
The oldest and most demanding tradition writes the specification in a logic and
proves the implementation refines it. seL4 is the canonical systems result: a
microkernel whose functional correctness is proved in Isabelle/HOL through two
refinement steps, from an abstract specification to an executable model to C
[3]. CompCert occupies the same ground for a compiler, in Coq.
.PP
Instruction-set architecture is where this tradition has quietly become
industrial. ARM publishes its architecture in machine-readable form, written in
its own ASL specification language \(em executable, and tested with the same
conformance suites ARM uses on silicon [4]. RISC-V has standardised an
executable formal specification of its ISA semantics in Sail [5], and Sail
models exist for ARMv8-A (derived automatically from the ARM-internal
specification), MIPS and CHERI-MIPS, complete enough to boot operating systems
[6].
.PP
What is generated here is telling: simulators, test generators, and theorem
prover input. The specification is not a convenience for producing boilerplate;
it is the definition of the artefact, and the implementations are the things
checked against it.
.
.S2 4.2  Model-driven engineering
.LP
Model-driven engineering, and its OMG-standardised form MDA, is the tradition
with the largest research literature and the worst reputation. The ambitious
version \(em platform-independent models transformed automatically into
production systems \(em did not arrive. The recorded reasons are consistent
across studies: round-trip synchronisation between model and code proved
painful, platform-specific detail was too numerous and too mobile to abstract
away, and large permissive metamodels were interpreted differently by different
tools.
.PP
The empirical picture is more interesting than the reputation. Whittle,
Hutchinson and Rouncefield surveyed 450 practitioners and interviewed 22 more,
and reported that MDE
.QS
\(lqmay be more widespread than commonly believed, but developers rarely use it
to generate whole systems; rather, they apply it to develop key parts of a
system often using domain-specific modeling languages developed specifically for
the purpose.\(rq [7]
.QE
.LP
Two findings follow from this that bear on the rest of the paper. The first is
that the modal successful application is small, local and bespoke \(em not a
platform, but one subsystem with a DSL written for it. The second, from the same
research group\(cqs industrial case studies, is that success and failure are
governed more by organisational and social factors than technical ones [8].
Tool maturity was widely reported as unsatisfactory, and productivity results
were mixed in both directions.
.
.S2 4.3  Interface definition languages
.LP
The most widely deployed form of the mechanism is the one nobody calls a
paradigm. Protocol Buffers, gRPC, OpenAPI, Smithy and WIT all take a schema as
authoritative and generate clients, servers, serialisers and documentation from
it, usually for several languages at once. The motivation is interoperability
rather than proof: two parties who cannot read each other\(cqs source can agree
on a schema.
.PP
This tradition is invisible as a paradigm precisely because it succeeded. It is
not adopted as a methodology; it arrives as a property of a framework, and the
generated directory is treated as build output rather than as a model.
.
.S2 4.4  In-project code generation
.LP
Between the schema and the model sits the case that is least written about: a
project defines its own small declarative language for one internal concern and
generates several artefacts from it at build time. LLVM\(cqs TableGen is the
mature example. Instruction definitions are written declaratively in \f(CW.td\fP
files, and backends extract from them to emit C++, documentation and
command-line option tables \(em explicitly, in the project\(cqs own description,
so that \(lqwe can produce many things based on that single source file\(rq [9].
.PP
The stated purpose is the removal of redundancy across near-identical
declarations. There is no proof obligation and no interoperability requirement.
The specification exists because writing the same fact in six places is a
maintenance hazard, and that is a sufficient reason on its own. This is the
tradition the case study in Section 9 belongs to, and the one Whittle\(cqs
finding predicts is both common and under-reported.
.
.S2 4.5  Agent-oriented specification-driven development
.LP
The 2025 wave described in Section 3.2 is the newest tradition and the only one
in which the generator is non-deterministic. This is a substantive difference
rather than an implementation detail. In the other four, regenerating from an
unchanged specification produces byte-identical output, which is what makes the
generated tree checkable in continuous integration. A generator that may produce
different code from the same specification cannot offer that, and the
consequences for review and for regression testing are still being worked out.
.
.\" ============================ 5 ============================
.S1 5.  How common is it?
.LP
Prevalence depends entirely on which tradition is counted, and the honest
composite answer is that the mechanism is near-universal while the methodology
is rare.
.PP
Interface definition is the most measurable. Postman\(cqs 2025 State of the API
report puts 83.2 per cent of respondents at some level of API-first adoption,
with 25 per cent describing themselves as fully API-first \(em up from 74 per
cent and 66 per cent reporting \(lqAPI first\(rq in the two preceding years [10].
These are self-reported figures from a vendor survey of a self-selected
population, and \(lqAPI-first\(rq is a broad label; they are useful as a
direction of travel rather than as a measurement. Even discounted heavily, they
describe a practice that is normal rather than exotic.
.PP
Model-driven engineering is the tradition where the reputation and the evidence
diverge most sharply. The survey position \(em more widespread than believed,
but applied to parts rather than wholes, frequently through purpose-built
DSLs [7] \(em means published adoption figures systematically undercount it,
because the practitioners are not using a tool with a name that a survey would
ask about. A team that writes a YAML file and a generator has done MDE, and will
not say so.
.PP
In-project generation is, for the same reason, effectively uncounted. It has no
vendor, no conference track and no survey instrument. Its prevalence can only be
inferred from artefacts \(em the
.CW zz_generated
prefixes, the
.CW .td
files, the \(lqdo not edit\(rq banners \(em which are ubiquitous in
infrastructure code and invisible in adoption statistics.
.PP
Agent-oriented SDD is too young for prevalence data of any quality. The
investment is real and the tooling is being used, but at the time of writing the
only disinterested assessment available is an \(lqAssess\(rq rating with
usability caveats.
.
.\" ============================ 6 ============================
.S1 6.  Which languages?
.LP
The choice of implementation language correlates with the tradition, not with
the paradigm \(em and, more usefully, with one property of the ecosystem.
.sp 0.5v
.TS
allbox tab(|);
lb  lb          lb
l   l           l.
Tradition|Specification written in|Target languages
_
Formal refinement|Isabelle/HOL, Coq, Sail, ASL|C, Haskell, OCaml
Model-driven engineering|UML, EMF/Ecore, bespoke DSLs|T{
Java (Eclipse tooling);
C from Simulink in embedded
T}
Interface definition|T{
Protobuf, OpenAPI, Smithy, WIT
T}|T{
Deliberately many: Go, Java,
C++, Python, TypeScript, Rust
T}
In-project generation|Project-specific DSLs|T{
C++ (TableGen), Go (Kubernetes),
Rust
T}
Agent-oriented SDD|Structured natural language|T{
Agnostic in principle;
TypeScript and Python in practice
T}
.TE
.sp 0.5v
.LP
The pattern worth extracting is that the determining factor is whether a
language\(cqs ecosystem has a first-class, conventional place for build-time
generated source. Go has it culturally, through
.CW "go generate"
and a strong convention of committing generated files with a marker comment.
Rust has it structurally, through
.CW build.rs
and module inclusion. C++ acquired it project by project, TableGen being the
best-known instance. Where that convention is absent, generated code tends to be
either checked in and then hand-edited \(em which destroys the property the
generation existed to provide \(em or regenerated inconsistently across
developer machines.
.PP
No language in the survey is intrinsically better suited than another. What
differs is how much ceremony the ecosystem imposes on the generated tree, and
therefore how likely a maintainer is to keep the discipline when it becomes
inconvenient.
.
.\" ============================ 7 ============================
.S1 7.  A cross-cutting axis: the direction of derivation
.LP
\(lqSingle source of truth\(rq is stated as though the source were always a
human-written description from which machine artefacts flow. In practice the
derivation runs in both directions, and which direction a project chooses
changes what can go wrong.
.PP
Kubernetes is the instructive counter-example. Its API types are Go structs
annotated with markers such as
.CW +k8s:deepcopy-gen ,
and the generators produce deep-copy implementations, typed clients and \(em
notably \(em the OpenAPI schema itself [11]. The schema is an output. The
authoritative statement of the API is the annotated Go source, which is
also the implementation.
.PP
This is
.I code-first ,
and it inverts the trade. A code-first tree cannot suffer the transcription
failure described in Section 8, because there is no transcription step: the
implementation is the specification. What it gives up is the ability to state
the rule in a vocabulary independent of the language it is implemented in, and
with it the ability to generate an implementation for a second language, or to
review the rule without reading Go.
.PP
Schema-first \(em xtgeoip, protobuf, OpenAPI-as-contract \(em buys that
independence and pays for it with a transcription boundary. Neither direction is
correct in general. The relevant observation is that the phrase \(lqsingle source
of truth\(rq describes both, and conceals the difference between them.
.
.\" ============================ 8 ============================
.S1 8.  The shared failure mode
.S2 8.1  What derivation guarantees
.LP
The guarantee a derived tree provides is precise and worth stating exactly,
because it is narrower than the slogan. If every artefact is generated from one
description by a deterministic generator, then no two artefacts can disagree
with each other. Documentation cannot drift from behaviour; the test matrix
cannot assert something the parser does not do; the error message in the manual
cannot be one the binary no longer emits. Drift is not merely discouraged, it is
inexpressible.
.PP
This is a real and substantial property. Nearly all of the maintenance burden
that motivates the mechanism \(em in TableGen, in protobuf, in a project\(cqs own
build-time DSL \(em is drift, and drift is genuinely eliminated. Nothing in what
follows should be read as diminishing that.
.
.S2 8.2  What it does not guarantee
.LP
The guarantee is
.I conditional
on the specification, and says nothing about it. Every artefact in the tree
agrees with the root because it was computed from the root. If the root is
wrong, the tree is uniformly, consistently, provably wrong \(em and the property
that makes the architecture valuable is exactly the property that removes the
evidence.
.PP
Consider what normally reveals a defect of this kind. In a hand-maintained
codebase the manual page and the parser are written by different people at
different times in different vocabularies. When they disagree, someone notices.
That disagreement is noise \(em it is the drift the architecture exists to
abolish \(em but it is also
.I signal ,
and abolishing the noise abolishes the signal with it. Worse, the defect is not
merely undetected but amplified: after regeneration, six artefacts assert the
wrong rule where previously one did, and each of them will be cited in good
faith as confirmation of the others.
.PP
The boundary this exposes is the authoring step. Derivation guarantees fidelity
.I downstream
of the specification and guarantees nothing about fidelity
.I into
it. The root of the tree is hand-written, and it is the one artefact in the
system that nothing checks.
.
.S2 8.3  The N-version precedent
.LP
This structure is not new, though it does not appear to have been named for
derived artefact trees. It is the mirror image of a result established forty
years ago in fault-tolerant computing.
.PP
N-version programming proposed that several teams independently implement the
same specification, with a voter selecting among their outputs at run time. The
reliability argument depended entirely on the versions failing independently.
Eckhardt and Lee showed theoretically that such independence cannot be assumed
as a matter of principle [12], and Knight and Leveson tested it: 27 versions,
written independently at two universities, subjected to one million inputs. The
individual versions were highly reliable, but the number of inputs on which more
than one version failed was substantially greater than independence would
predict [13]. Independence of failure could not, in general, be assumed.
.PP
A significant part of the reason is that the versions shared one specification.
Where a specification is silent, ambiguous, or wrong, every implementation
derived from it inherits the same defect, and no amount of implementation
diversity recovers from a common-mode fault in the thing they all read.
.PP
A specification-driven tree reaches the same place from the opposite direction.
N-version programming assumed an independence it did not have; a derived tree
discards independence deliberately, and receives consistency in exchange. That
is a defensible and usually a good trade \(em consistency is what most projects
actually need, and machine-checked reliability is not on offer at this price
anyway. But the trade should be made with the consequence in view: the tree
inherits the known common-mode failure without the mitigation, and it has one
specification rather than N, which is the maximally correlated case.
.
.S2 8.4  Not a validation failure
.LP
It is tempting to file this under Boehm\(cqs distinction between verification
(\(lqare we building the product right?\(rq) and validation (\(lqare we building
the right product?\(rq) [14], and to conclude that generation buys the former
and never claimed the latter. That framing is close but not exact, and the
imprecision matters.
.PP
In the case examined in Section 9, nobody misunderstood the requirement. The
correct rule was written down, correctly, in two separate documents. It was lost
in transcription
.I into
the specification file. That is not a failure to determine what the product
should do; it is a fidelity failure at a translation boundary, of exactly the
kind that a compiler exists to prevent and that no compiler was watching here.
.PP
The sharper statement is therefore not \(lqgeneration does verification, not
validation,\(rq which invites the reply that validation was never its job. It is
that a derived tree
.B "verifies every edge except the first one" ,
and the first edge \(em human intent into machine-readable specification \(em is
where a transcription error is both most likely and least visible.
.
.\" ============================ 9 ============================
.S1 9.  Case study: a four-month agreement on a wrong rule
.LP
The following is drawn from the authors\(cq own project, a Rust utility that
builds binary geolocation databases. It is a small instance of tradition 4.4:
one declarative file,
.CW cli.yaml ,
defines the command-line semantics, and a generator emits the guard table, the
error constants, the manual page, two documentation formats and the integration
test corpus from it.
.
.S2 9.1  The defect
.LP
The tool accepts modifier flags whose meaning depends on context. One of them,
.CW -p
(\(lqprune\(rq), removes old archived files, and which family of files it
removes depends on what the invocation produced. In the
.CW run
context the program performs a remote fetch \(em producing a new compressed
source archive \(em and, if
.CW -b
is given, also produces a new binary backup. Both land in the same directory.
The combination
.CW "run -b -p"
therefore offers
.CW -p
two candidate targets and no way to choose between them.
.PP
The manual page said so. It had said so since it was written:
.QS
\(lqUsing \-p in a context where both fetch and backup are active (e.g.
.CW "run \-b \-p" )
is an error: the prune target is ambiguous. To prune both, run the program
twice.\(rq
.QE
.LP
The specification disagreed. It declared the same invocation valid, with a
determinate four-step plan, and the guard intended to reject the ambiguity
required three flags where two were sufficient \(em it demanded a
.CW -c
(\(lqclean\(rq) flag that has nothing whatever to do with choosing a prune
target. The binary accepted
.CW "run -b -p"
and silently pruned only one of the two families.
.
.S2 9.2  Why nothing caught it
.LP
The project is not short of checks. It has an exhaustive snapshot test pinning
the parser\(cqs behaviour over all 136 flag combinations; a generation-time
cross-check proving the documented examples agree with the guard table; a
contradiction test suite; and 193 unit tests. Every one of them passed
throughout.
.PP
They passed because they agreed. The specification said the combination was
valid; the guard table generated from it accepted the combination; the example
matrix generated from it documented the combination as valid; the manual\(cqs
example section, generated from it, listed the combination among the working
invocations; the test corpus, generated from it, asserted success. The snapshot
test recorded what the parser did \(em which was what the specification said \(em
and locked it in as the expected value. A snapshot pins what a function
.I is ,
not what it
.I "ought to be" ,
and so a wrong premise is not caught by one; it is ratified by one and
thereafter defended by it.
.PP
Six artefacts asserted the wrong rule. Not one of them constituted independent
evidence for any other, because all six were computed from the same file.
.
.S2 9.3  What did catch it
.LP
Two artefacts dissented, and both were hand-written prose that no test read. The
first was the manual page template quoted above. The second, discovered during
the investigation, was an enumeration of command-line behaviour predating the
specification file entirely, which states the rule in a different notation:
.CW "when: has: [b, p]"
\(em precisely the two-flag predicate that was eventually shipped as the fix.
.PP
This second document settles the character of the error. The intent was
recorded correctly, in a rule-shaped form, before the specification was
authored. The specification did not embody a different design decision; it
embodied a mis-transcription of an existing one, and the third flag entered
because the guard was patterned after a neighbouring guard where all targets
happen to be flag-driven. In
.CW run ,
one target is implicit \(em a remote fetch produces a new archive whether or not
any flag requested it \(em and the pattern silently did not transfer.
.
.S2 9.4  What the architecture did well
.LP
The corrective was correspondingly cheap, and this deserves equal emphasis. The
behavioural change was one line: the guard\(cqs required flag set. Regeneration
propagated it to six artefacts with no hand-editing. The blast radius was
predictable before the edit and confirmed exactly after it \(em two rows of the
136-combination snapshot moved and nothing else did. Guard precedence required
no reasoning, because the snapshot proved it rather than the author asserting it.
.PP
The generation step also refused an incoherent intermediate state. The
specification carries a proof obligation that each error case be demonstrated by
exactly one example, since that example is the sole declared link between an
error identifier and its message text. Tightening the guard caused two examples
to claim the same error, and code generation aborted rather than emit the
ambiguity \(em in a change whose subject was ambiguity. The forced resolution
was the correct one.
.PP
The manual cost of the change fell entirely on what was
.I not
yet derived: three hand-maintained corpus-size assertions in the integration
test binary. That is the accurate price of a semantic change under this
architecture \(em proportional to the un-derived remainder, not to the size of
the change.
.
.\" ============================ 10 ============================
.S1 10.  Practical consequences
.LP
Four recommendations follow, stated in decreasing order of confidence.
.
.BU
.B "Keep an independent restatement of the rules, and do not treat it as duplication."
A hand-written document that states the same rule in a different vocabulary is
the only oracle a derived tree can have for its own root. The instinct of a
single-source-of-truth architecture is to delete such documents as redundant, or
to generate them and thereby fold them into the tree. Both moves remove the last
independent witness. In the case study, the two artefacts that were right were
precisely the two that nothing generated and nothing checked.
.BE
.BU
.B "Check the root against the restatement, not just the tree against the root."
Consistency checks naturally grow downward, because the derived artefacts are
machine-readable and easy to compare. The check that was missing in the case
study was upward: nothing compared the manual\(cqs prose claims about invalid
combinations against the guard table that implements them. This is harder than
the checks that existed \(em prose is not a structured list \(em which is why it
had not been written, and why the defect survived a deliberate audit of the
manual page conducted two days earlier.
.BE
.BU
.B "Treat agreement among generated artefacts as one observation, not many."
A review that finds the schema, the documentation and the tests all in accord
has confirmed one fact: that the generator is deterministic. It has not
corroborated the rule. This is worth stating explicitly in review guidance,
because the psychological pull of six agreeing documents is considerable and
entirely unearned.
.BE
.BU
.B "Prefer specifications that are hard to mis-transcribe."
The defect in the case study was a predicate with one term too many, patterned
after a neighbouring rule whose shape did not transfer. Specification formats
that force the author to state
.I why
a rule holds \(em or that derive the rule from the structure it constrains,
rather than restating it \(em reduce the surface on which this error is
possible. This is the least evidenced of the four recommendations and is offered
as a direction rather than a technique.
.BE
.
.\" ============================ 11 ============================
.S1 11.  Gaps in the literature
.LP
Three gaps became apparent while assembling this survey.
.PP
.B "The common case is the least studied."
Formal refinement and model-driven engineering have substantial literatures.
Interface definition has extensive practitioner documentation. The in-project
build-time DSL \(em one project, one small language, several generated artefacts,
no vendor and no methodology \(em has almost none, despite the survey evidence
that this is where most successful model-driven practice actually resides [7].
The absence is self-reinforcing: because the practice has no name, its
practitioners do not identify as a community, and nothing is written for them.
.PP
.B "The failure mode has a theory in the wrong field."
Common-mode failure arising from a shared specification is well understood in
fault-tolerant computing [12][13], and appears not to have been transferred to
derived artefact trees, where the correlation is total rather than partial and
therefore stronger. The recent literature on code and tests generated together
by a language model has independently rediscovered the same principle \(em that
an oracle derived from the generator is not an oracle \(em which suggests the
general form is worth stating once, in terms that cover all three cases.
.PP
.B "Direction of derivation is under-theorised."
Schema-first and code-first architectures are routinely compared on ergonomics
and tooling, and rarely on what each makes impossible. A code-first tree cannot
suffer a transcription failure into the specification, because there is no such
step; it pays for that with the loss of language-independent expression of the
rule. This is a genuine trade with consequences for review, portability and
failure modes, and it is largely absent from the discussion that the phrase
\(lqsingle source of truth\(rq is used to close.
.
.\" ============================ 12 ============================
.S1 12.  Conclusion
.LP
Specification-driven architecture is a real and widely practised mechanism with
five separate lineages, no unified literature, and a name that currently
denotes the youngest and least proven of the five. Its central guarantee \(em
that derived artefacts cannot disagree \(em is genuine and valuable, and is
narrower than it is usually taken to be. It holds downstream of the
specification and nowhere else.
.PP
The specification itself is written by a person, checked by nothing, and
believed by everything. Where that authoring step goes wrong, the architecture
does not merely fail to help; it removes the disagreement that would have
revealed the error and multiplies the artefacts that assert it. The mitigation
is not more derivation. It is deliberately retained redundancy: a hand-written
statement of the same rules in a different vocabulary, and a check that compares
the two. That is an unfashionable conclusion for a single-source-of-truth
architecture to reach, and it is what the evidence supports.
.
